Don't change this PR for this! 😄  but for future changes the switch pattern syntax can be more compact? I think it's something like

```c#
    string message = error switch
    {
        RegexParseError.Unknown => SR.Format(SR.MakeException, pattern, offset, SR.Generic),
        RegexParseError.AlternationHasTooManyConditions => SR.Format(SR.MakeException, pattern, offset, SR.AlternationHasTooManyConditions),
        //...
    };
```

Now not on phone so I can read properly, sorry about that. We have to implement exactly the shape approved or go back for re-approval if we discover it was a mistake.

As you say many of the error messages we have expect a parameter, but the constructor the API review requested doesn't take that parameter. What message are we supposed to put in the exception for eg "UndefinedBackref" if the message is "Reference to undefined group number {0}." and we don't have the replacement? I think it has to be a generic message, and we won't use this constructor anyway - maybe nobody will.

How about this? 
```c#
// public ctor we don't use -- SR.RegexParseError is `Invalid regular expression pattern. Error '{0}' at offset {1}` or something
// and it would make a message like `Invalid regular expression pattern. Error 'UnterminatedComment' at offset '7'." 
public RegexParseException(RegexParseError error, int offset) : base(SR.Format(SR.RegexParseError, error, offset)
{
      Error = error;
      Offset = offset;
}
internal RegexParseException(RegexParseError error, int offset, string pattern, string param = null) : base(MakeMessage(error, offset, pattern, param)
{
      Error = error;
      Offset = offset;
}
private static string MakeMessage(RegexParseError error, int offset, string pattern, string param)
{
    string resource = error switch
    {
        RegexParseError.UnterminatedComment => SR.UnterminatedComment,
        RegexParseError.AlternationHasMalformedCondition => SR.IllegalCondition
        //...
    };
    string innerMessage = SR.Format(resource, param);
    return SR.Format(SR.MakeException, pattern, offset, innerMessage);
}
```
Would that work?

Another thought -- we generally use the "throw helper" pattern in code that might be "hot" because the generated code to construct and throw an exception object is significantly larger than an unconditional call to a throw helper.

I doubt that the regex parser is particularly "hot" but it already has a throw helper for it own purposes, it would not be unreasonable to write it like those throw helpers eg.,

```c#
class RegexParser
{
    // ... parsing
    ThrowRegexParseException(error);

   // elsewhere, ThrowRegexParseException(error, param);

...
   private void ThrowRegexParseException(RegexParseError error) => ThrowRegexParseException(error, null); // overload, not default param, so it's not visible to caller

   private void ThrowRegexParseException(RegexParseError error, string param)
   {
          throw new RegexParseException(error, _currentPos, _pattern, param);
    }
```
